## Agent skills

Skills for operating the bucket (scaffolding manifests, and the checks a
generated field needs) come from
[`scoop-bucket-skills`](https://github.com/hasansezertasan/scoop-bucket-skills),
which this repository does not ship.

## Add a manifest

Manifests are scaffolded by the `scoop-add` skill from
[`scoop-bucket-skills`](https://github.com/hasansezertasan/scoop-bucket-skills),
which this repository does not ship. That repository is a skills repository
(`skills/<name>/SKILL.md`) that also installs as a Claude Code or Codex plugin.
Install it for your agent and ask it to add a package; the skill's `SKILL.md`
documents the routes and options.

Review inferred descriptions, licenses, archive layout, and commands before
committing. The Windows workflow defaults to `<command> version`; add an entry to
the `$smoke` map in `.github/workflows/tests.yml` when a package uses a different
smoke test.

## Validate changes

```sh
mise install
mise run check      # tests, catalog freshness, YAML/Actions lint, Cobo
mise run lint       # every prek hook, across the whole repository
mise run style      # yamlfmt, yamllint, actionlint, action-validator, zizmor
```

`prek.toml` is the commit-time gate — `mise run hooks` installs it. It covers
file hygiene, `typos`, `markdownlint-cli2`, and the same YAML and Actions
toolchain `mise run style` runs without a hook install; the overlap is
deliberate so neither path is required.

CI additionally validates every manifest and installs each non-placeholder
package on Windows.

## Automated updates

The weekly workflow checks every manifest and opens a pull request when upstream
versions change. A package repository can request an immediate update after a
release with a `repository_dispatch` event of type `update-manifest`:

```json
{"package":"example","version":"1.2.3"}
```

`package` is required and selects a manifest family, so `example` also selects
`example-pipx`. `version` is optional and informational because the updater
re-checks the configured upstream source. A producer needs a fine-grained token
with **Contents: write** access to this bucket solely to call the dispatch API.
Store that token in the producer repository, not in the bucket.

Automated pull requests use the repository secret `WORKFLOW_TOKEN` if defined,
falling back to `GITHUB_TOKEN`. Because GitHub does not start workflows for
actions performed by `GITHUB_TOKEN`, a pull request opened with it does not run
`Tests` or the other checks. Run the `Tests` workflow manually via
`workflow_dispatch` on the update branch before merging, or push to its branch,
or close and reopen the pull request — or configure a fine-grained personal
access token or GitHub App token for this repository as `WORKFLOW_TOKEN`
(**Contents: write** and **Pull requests: write**) so the checks start
automatically. The secret is optional.

`zizmor.yml` audits the bucket's own workflows on every push and pull request
and uploads findings to the Security tab. The dispatch receivers act on
attacker-controllable payloads with a write token, so keep it green.
`gitignore-drift.yml` re-renders the Cobo-managed `.gitignore` weekly and opens
a pull request when upstream boilerplate drifted. It opens its pull request the
same way the update workflows do.

## Pull request conventions

`check-pr-title.yml` requires
[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) titles
and `check-branch-name.yml` requires
[Conventional Branch](https://conventional-branch.github.io/) names, both
reported as a sticky comment on the pull request. `renovate/*` and the
`auto-update/*` branches the bump workflows push are exempt.

## Managed boilerplate

`.gitignore` is generated from GitHub's macOS, Windows, Linux, Visual Studio Code,
and Python templates with [Cobo](https://github.com/hasansezertasan/cobo). Its
exact inputs are recorded in `cobo.lock`.

```sh
cobo check --strict
cobo sync
```

Do not edit Cobo-managed blocks by hand; update their inputs and re-render them.

